Gas Flow Control - Technical Handoff
Project: Gas Flow Control
Release: v1.2
Prepared by: Andrew Sharp
Status: Software-complete prototype; physical valve/sensor integration pending 
1. Current Project Status
The Gas Flow Control software platform is substantially complete and ready for handoff.
Completed and tested:
•	Flutter user interface
•	Secure sign-in using employee email and 6-digit PIN
•	Six independent controller/tool positions
•	Remote valve-position commands
•	Individual valve close
•	Close All Valves
•	Home/system-status screen
•	Command history
•	NORMAL, ADJUSTING, WARNING, FAULT, and OFFLINE states
•	Offline Demo Mode
•	iPhone Home Screen installation
•	Firebase web deployment
•	Raspberry Pi 4 controller integration
•	Raspberry Pi automatic controller startup
•	Connection-loss and recovery behavior
•	Hardware abstraction layer for future real valve control
The remaining work is primarily physical hardware integration, calibration, and field validation.
2. Current System Architecture
Software / Control Path
Gas Flow Control App
→ FastAPI backend
→ Raspberry Pi 4 Model B
→ Gas Controller API
→ Valve Hardware Interface
→ Physical valve hardware
The current Raspberry Pi hardware layer is simulated so the rest of the system can be tested without a physical valve.
3. Raspberry Pi Controller
Current controller hostname:
GasControlPi
Controller API port:
9000
Linux service:
gas-controller.service
The service is configured with systemd and starts automatically when the Raspberry Pi boots.
Check controller status:
sudo systemctl status gas-controller
Expected result:
Loaded: loaded (... enabled ...)
Active: active (running)
Controller health can be checked from another device on the same network:
Invoke-RestMethod http://PI_IP_ADDRESS:9000/health
Expected response includes:
ok          : True
controller  : GasControlPi
hardware    : simulated-valve
tool_count  : 6
4. Raspberry Pi Software Structure
The Raspberry Pi controller currently uses:
controller.py
valve_hardware.py
controller.py provides the network/API layer.
valve_hardware.py separates valve hardware behavior from the API.
Current implementation:
SimulatedValveHardware
Future implementation should replace this with a physical hardware driver, for example:
EnfieldValveHardware
The goal is to replace only the hardware layer without redesigning:
•	Flutter UI
•	Main API
•	Raspberry Pi controller routes
•	Tool/status models
5. Recommended Physical Hardware Direction
Current preferred one-valve architecture:
Raspberry Pi 4
→ Sequent Microsystems SM-I-001 Industrial Automation HAT
→ 0-10 V command
→ Enfield PFV Proportional Solenoid Valve with onboard linear driver
This approach avoids a separate stepper motor and stepper driver.
Preferred valve characteristics:
•	24 VDC
•	0-10 V command
•	Normally closed
•	Proportional control
•	Stainless construction where appropriate
•	Integrated actuator/driver
6. Information Required Before Final Hardware Selection
Before ordering the exact valve, pressure sensor, and flow sensor, confirm:
•	Normal argon flow in CFH
•	Minimum argon flow
•	Maximum argon flow
•	Regulated pressure entering the valve in PSI
•	Existing regulator/flowmeter model
•	Existing tubing/fitting sizes
•	Installation environment
•	Required behavior during power loss
•	Required manual gas isolation method
These values determine the exact valve orifice, calibration pressure, sensor ranges, and gas-side fittings.
7. Recommended First Physical Test
Do not begin with gas.
First prove the control signal:
App
→ Raspberry Pi
→ Sequent I/O
→ Digital Multimeter
Expected output:
•	0% ≈ 0.0 V
•	25% ≈ 2.5 V
•	50% ≈ 5.0 V
•	75% ≈ 7.5 V
•	100% ≈ 10.0 V
After this passes:
1.	Connect one proportional valve electrically.
2.	Verify physical valve response without gas.
3.	Test Close Valve.
4.	Test Close All.
5.	Test controller restart.
6.	Test communication loss and recovery.
7.	Add real pressure sensing.
8.	Add real argon flow sensing.
9.	Calibrate.
10.	Proceed to controlled gas testing.
8. Software Release / Web App
Current live application:
https://gas-flow-control-demo.web.app
Current release:
v1.2
The app can be added to an iPhone Home Screen.
Demo Mode has been tested for offline operation after the app is first installed and cached.




9. Source Code / Version Control
Repository:
https://github.com/asharpharder/gas_flow_control
Primary branch:
master
Typical update workflow:
git pull origin master
After approved changes:
git add .
git commit -m "Describe change"
git push origin master
Before handoff or release:
git status
Desired result:
nothing to commit, working tree clean
10. Flutter Web Build / Deployment
Typical release build:
cd flutter_app
flutter clean
flutter pub get
flutter analyze
Build web app:
flutter build web --no-web-resources-cdn --dart-define=DEMO_MODE=true --dart-define=OPERATOR_ID=field-review --dart-define=ACCESS_PIN=YOUR_PIN --dart-define=HARDER_EMAIL_DOMAIN=YOUR_DOMAIN
Deploy through Firebase:
cd ..
firebase.cmd deploy --only hosting
11. Authentication
Current prototype access control uses:
•	Employee email domain validation
•	Configurable 6-digit PIN
The PIN and email domain are supplied at build time using --dart-define.
This is appropriate for prototype/review access but should be replaced with centralized authentication before a production deployment.
12. Current Limitations
The software is ready for physical hardware integration, but the overall gas-control system is not yet production validated.
Still required:
•	Final valve selection
•	Final pressure sensor selection
•	Final flow sensor selection
•	Electrical integration
•	Calibration
•	Gas compatibility verification
•	Pressure-rating verification
•	Controlled gas testing
•	Fail-safe validation
•	Field trials
•	Safety/engineering review




13. Recommended Continuation Plan
Phase 1 - One-Valve Electrical Prototype
•	Install industrial I/O HAT
•	Verify 0-10 V commands
•	Connect one proportional valve
•	Implement physical valve driver
•	Validate movement and shutdown behavior
Phase 2 - Sensor Integration
•	Add pressure transducer
•	Add argon flow sensor
•	Convert sensor signals into PSI and CFH
•	Integrate readings into existing telemetry
Phase 3 - Bench Validation
•	Calibrate valve position/flow relationship
•	Establish warning/fault thresholds
•	Test network and hardware failures
•	Perform controlled argon testing
Phase 4 - Field Pilot
•	Install one validated controller
•	Gather operator feedback
•	Verify reliability
•	Expand only after successful testing
14. Handoff Recommendation
The software should remain feature-frozen unless testing identifies a specific operational need.
The next engineering effort should focus on:
physical valve control, sensor integration, calibration, and field validation
rather than additional UI development.
The system has been intentionally structured so that future hardware can be added beneath the existing Raspberry Pi hardware interface without rewriting the completed user interface or network architecture.

